{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":233847,"duration_api_ms":230685,"num_turns":40,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"177744b6-ddb3-4a91-a250-36a5277731c0","total_cost_usd":2.01879425,"usage":{"input_tokens":8072,"cache_creation_input_tokens":109591,"cache_read_input_tokens":2173381,"output_tokens":8272,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":109591,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":8072,"outputTokens":8272,"cacheReadInputTokens":2173381,"cacheCreationInputTokens":109591,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.01879425,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"CleanFilter action (engine.rs:353-373) reduces microbe biomass by `intensity * 0.5` across all four guilds but does not route the removed biomass to dissolved organics or detritus. The element content (N and C) of the cleaned-off biomass silently vanishes. The budget guard masks this because `action:clean_filter` is listed as an open-system action in `tick_has_closed_system_nitrogen`. Fix: route the setback biomass to dissolved organic pools (same pattern as `route_live_biomass_to_dissolved_organics` in nitrogen_cycle.rs), and either remove `action:clean_filter` from the open-system exclusion list so the guard can verify conservation, or record an explicit export entry in the budget ledger.","rationale":"This is a genuine mass conservation gap. The acceptance criteria state that 'the budget ledger should show the water change as an explicit export, not as an unexplained loss' — the same principle applies to filter cleaning. Without routing, any test that measures long-term N/C drift after filter cleaning will see unaccounted loss. Downstream dependents (tanksim-6e5.3.4 end-to-end bookkeeping audit) will flag this."},{"body":"The `test_total_n_helper_sums_all_pools` test (budget_tracking.rs:242-274) sums microbe biomass into a single `microbe_biomass_g` value and calls `manual_live_biomass_nitrogen_mg(microbe_biomass_g, ratio)` once, while `nitrogen_budget_components` computes N for each microbe guild individually. Because `live_biomass_nitrogen_mg` applies `max(0.0)` per guild and the organic_nitrogen formula `mass * 1000 * ratio / (1+ratio)` is linear, the results coincide only when all guilds are non-negative. The test happens to pass because the fixture values are all positive. A test with one guild at exactly 0.0 would still pass due to linearity, but the structural approach of testing the sum rather than each component separately means the test does not verify that each guild is individually accounted. Consider splitting the microbe budget assertion to check per-guild contributions, mirroring the actual component structure.","rationale":"The test's correctness relies on linearity of the conversion function, not on structural coverage of each guild. If a future refactor introduces per-guild conversion that is non-linear (e.g., a minimum biomass threshold), the aggregated test would mask missing guilds. This is a minor testing robustness concern, not a current bug."},{"body":"The `dissolved_feed_residue_g_total` bookkeeping field is decremented only by mineralization (nitrogen_cycle.rs:120-121) and water changes (water_change.rs:60), but not by `SiphonDetritus` (engine.rs:348-351) or `TrimPlants` (engine.rs:334-346). After siphoning, `dissolved_feed_residue_g_total` may exceed the sum of actual DOC+DON it shadows. While this field is excluded from the budget and is only used for filter clogging heuristics (nitrogen_cycle.rs:600), it can produce stale clogging signals post-siphon. Consider decrementing `dissolved_feed_residue_g_total` proportionally during siphoning, consistent with the other retained-fraction reductions already done for particulate and fine detritus.","rationale":"Stale clogging signal after siphoning could confuse players or cause incorrect filter behavior. The fix is trivial (one line) and aligns with the existing pattern in water_change.rs:60."}],"summary":"Reviewed all budget tracking infrastructure: BudgetLedger, BudgetSnapshot, canonical total_nitrogen/total_carbon helpers, per-system stage recording in the engine, the budget guard, and all 17 budget tracking tests (all passing). The implementation is solid — the fixed-array component model with structural coverage test, the opt-in ledger with zero cost when disabled, the open/closed system classification for the budget guard, and the per-element gross in/out tracking via BudgetSnapshot diffs are all well designed. Found three actionable issues: (1) CleanFilter silently drops microbe biomass N/C without routing or export recording — a genuine conservation gap; (2) the total_n test aggregates microbe guilds before checking, which is fragile under future non-linear conversions; (3) dissolved_feed_residue bookkeeping drifts after siphon actions, producing stale clogging signals."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"2d54d53f-9bbe-4b9c-b362-b6da152210ea"}
